<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emmanuel Endeley</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1916">1916</a>–<a href="/years/1988">1988</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emmanuel Endeley was a Cameroonian physician and politician who led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-cameroons">Southern Cameroons</a> in British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroons</a>. He headed the territory's government from 1954 and became its first premier in 1958, favouring close ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>. He lost the 1959 election to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ngu-foncha">John Ngu Foncha</a>, whose party backed reunification with Cameroon.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emmanuel-endeley.jpg" alt="Emmanuel Endeley" width="148" height="186" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Prabook · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:E._M._L._Endeley.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Emmanuel Mbela Lifafa Endeley was born on 10 April 1916 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buea">Buea</a>, then in German Kamerun, to Mathias Lifafa and Mariana Mojoko Endeley; his family were wealthy members of the Bakweri people and his father was a Bakweri chief. He was educated at a government school in Buea, a Catholic mission school in Bonjongo and Government College, Umuahia, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, and won a scholarship in 1935 to the Nigerian School of Medicine in Yaba. He joined the colonial medical service in 1942 and worked in Buea, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a> and Port Harcourt.</p><p>As a medical student in 1939 he helped form the Cameroon Youth League in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a>, becoming its general secretary. Back in the British Cameroons he built up the workers' union of the Cameroon Development Corporation, becoming its president in 1949, petitioned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> delegations and organised general strikes. In 1949 he also founded the Cameroons National Federation, later the Cameroons National Congress.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Elected to the Eastern Nigerian Assembly in Enugu in 1951, Endeley was Minister of Labour between 1953 and 1954. He led the Southern Cameroonian representatives out of the assembly and negotiated the creation of the autonomous region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-cameroons">Southern Cameroons</a> in 1954. In 1953 he joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ngu-foncha">John Ngu Foncha</a> and Solomon Tandeng Muna in founding the Kamerun National Congress, which sought autonomy for the territory.</p><p>His views later turned towards closer integration with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>. Foncha and Muna broke away in 1955 to form the Kamerun National Democratic Party, and Endeley allied his party with the pro-Nigeria Kamerun People's Party. In May 1960 the two merged as the Cameroon People's National Convention, which opposed reunification with French Cameroun; Endeley urged a no vote in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> plebiscite of 11 February 1961, but the vote went for reunification.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In the federal state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a>, Endeley led the opposition to Foncha in West Cameroon and supported President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmadou-ahidjo">Ahmadou Ahidjo</a>'s moves towards a one-party system. In 1965 he became leader of government business for West Cameroon. He sat on the central committee of the Cameroon National Union, presided over its Fako section from 1966 to 1985 and was elected to the National Assembly. He died in June 1988.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “E. M. L. Endeley” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1954 the Southern Cameroons gained its own legislature, with <b>Emmanuel Endeley</b> and later John Ngu Foncha as premier.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-cameroons">British Cameroons</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buea">Buea</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/cameroon">Cameroon</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=4.17,9.23&amp;year=1962" aria-label="Buea on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4806 2008 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Emmanuel Endeley"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5256 2278h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5256 2278h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=4.17,9.23&amp;year=1962">What was here in 1962? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
